WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:07.160
Usually I write queries in order to do data analysis.

00:07.160 --> 00:10.080
So in that analytics you have always like starting points.

00:10.080 --> 00:13.200
You have like a topic that you are analyzing like the customer.

00:13.200 --> 00:15.400
So you have always like a master table.

00:15.400 --> 00:18.360
So I always start with the main table of my analysis.

00:18.360 --> 00:22.680
So in my query I start from this table from table A the main table.

00:22.680 --> 00:23.840
And then what happens.

00:23.880 --> 00:25.160
The data is not enough.

00:25.160 --> 00:29.960
In this table I need some extra data that comes from another table like the table B.

00:29.960 --> 00:34.120
So the table B is only here like an additional data to the master table.

00:34.120 --> 00:40.240
So I go and use the left join in order to connect the table B and then I find another interesting information

00:40.240 --> 00:41.920
in another table in table C.

00:41.960 --> 00:43.160
So same thing happens.

00:43.160 --> 00:46.560
I go and join the tables using the left join and so on.

00:46.560 --> 00:50.360
So I keep connecting multiple tables to this main table in the middle.

00:50.360 --> 00:52.240
And my query is going to look like this.

00:52.240 --> 00:54.960
Always doing left joins with multiple tables.

00:55.000 --> 00:59.800
Now of course you might say yeah but sometimes you would like to see only the matching data and so on.

00:59.800 --> 01:02.080
So it makes sense only to use the inner join.

01:02.080 --> 01:07.280
Well, in order to do that, I can control everything that I want to see in the final results using

01:07.280 --> 01:08.280
the Where clause.

01:08.280 --> 01:12.440
So in the Where clause, I define exactly what I want to see in the final results.

01:12.440 --> 01:17.320
So with that I get like more flexibility on whether I want to see the matching and matching data and

01:17.320 --> 01:18.880
so on like we've done in the lift.

01:18.880 --> 01:19.480
Antijoin.

01:19.480 --> 01:19.880
Right.

01:19.880 --> 01:25.720
So as I'm analyzing data, I tend very frequently having this setup where I start from the main table

01:25.720 --> 01:31.040
and I lift join all other tables, and with the where conditions I control the final results.

01:31.040 --> 01:33.800
So this is how I connect multiple tables together.

01:33.800 --> 01:37.400
So now if I want to visualize this in like circles it's going to look like this.

01:37.400 --> 01:38.560
We have the circle A.

01:38.600 --> 01:44.520
So this is the master table the starting point I want to see all the data from table A and I live join

01:44.520 --> 01:45.600
it in with another table.

01:45.640 --> 01:49.200
Table B and from table B I want to see only the matching data.

01:49.200 --> 01:50.560
So it's like the live join.

01:50.600 --> 01:53.200
Now what's gonna happen I'm gonna go and add another table.

01:53.200 --> 01:55.320
So another circle the circle C.

01:55.360 --> 01:58.800
And from the circle C we want to see only the matching data.

01:58.800 --> 02:01.440
And of course you can keep adding circles to this.

02:01.440 --> 02:03.040
But it's going to be always the same thing.

02:03.080 --> 02:05.800
And in your circle has only the matching data.

02:05.800 --> 02:11.040
So now as we learned, we can use joins in order to combine multiple tables to get a complete big picture

02:11.040 --> 02:16.160
about topics like the customers, I would like to see everything about the customers in the final results.

02:16.160 --> 02:20.080
So either you're going to do it like me, where you start from the main table and then go and left,

02:20.080 --> 02:21.440
join all other tables.

02:21.440 --> 02:25.360
Or maybe you say, you know what, there is no main table about the customers data.

02:25.400 --> 02:27.640
All the tables are equally important.

02:27.680 --> 02:33.480
Then you can go and join all those tables using the inner join if you are interested only on the match

02:33.480 --> 02:33.920
data.

02:33.920 --> 02:38.800
So what can happen if you have again those circles from the A, you need only the matching data from

02:38.840 --> 02:42.840
B, you need as well only matching data and as well from the third circle.

02:42.840 --> 02:46.720
So you are interested only on the overlapping between all those three tables.

02:46.720 --> 02:51.480
So you will get only this section where you have overlapping between all three tables.

02:51.480 --> 02:54.920
So this is of course another way on how to join multiple tables.

02:54.960 --> 02:55.160
Okay.

